The Iris dataset contains 150 samples of iris flowers from three species: Setosa, Versicolor, 
and Virginica (50 each). Each sample has four numerical features measured in cm: sepal length, sepal width, petal length, 
and petal width. There are no missing values. Setosa is easily separable due to its small petals, while Versicolor 
and Virginica overlap slightly. The dataset is widely used for classification, clustering, and visualization tasks. 
It was introduced by Ronald Fisher in 1936 and remains a standard benchmark for machine learning algorithms due to its simplicity and balanced class distribution.

